# Discord Soundboard

A Dockerized Discord voice bot with a web control panel. It can scan audio from
multiple folders, stream YouTube audio, and accept commands from Discord users
who do not have access to the website.

## Current features

- Separate **Sounds** and **YouTube** pages in the web UI
- Sounds page auto-detects collections and nested subfolders for browsing
- Sort sounds by name, play count or date added; group by folder or show one
  flat list; star favourites to pin them to the top (saved per browser)
- Volume and Normalize controls in the top bar apply to the next sound and to
  whatever is playing; volume changes take effect instantly
- Recursive scanning of multiple audio directories
- Discord server and voice-channel selection in the UI
- Optional always-on voice recording: save the last minute of voice chat as
  a clip, with each speaker's loudness evened out automatically
- Discord slash commands with sound-name autocomplete
- YouTube URL streaming through `yt-dlp` and FFmpeg
- Stop/disconnect controls and optional web API-key protection

## Set up Discord

1. Open the [Discord Developer Portal](https://discord.com/developers/applications),
   create an application, and add a bot.
2. Copy the bot token. Do not commit or share it.
3. In **OAuth2 → URL Generator**, select the `bot` and `applications.commands`
   scopes. Give the bot these permissions: View Channels, Send Messages,
   Connect, and Speak.
4. Open the generated URL to invite the bot to your server.

Message Content Intent is not needed because the bot uses slash commands.

## Run with Docker Compose

For deployment on a separate Linux Docker server or Proxmox LXC, follow
[`DEPLOY.md`](DEPLOY.md). The short version after copying the project is:

```text
bash deploy.sh
```

1. Copy `.env.example` to `.env` and set `DISCORD_TOKEN` and
   `WEB_API_TOKEN`.
2. Put audio files in `./sounds`.
3. Start the app:

   ```text
   docker compose up --build -d
   ```

4. Open `http://localhost:8000`. Use the gear button to enter the same
   `WEB_API_TOKEN` stored in `.env`.

The API key is kept in browser session storage, so it is forgotten when that
browser session ends. If this UI is exposed outside your private network, put it
behind HTTPS and an authentication-aware reverse proxy as well.

## Use multiple audio folders

Mount every host folder read-only in `compose.yaml`:

```yaml
volumes:
  - ./sounds:/media/sounds:ro
  - D:/Shared/Memes:/media/memes:ro
  - D:/Shared/Intros:/media/intros:ro
```

Then list the paths *inside the container* in `.env`:

```dotenv
AUDIO_DIRECTORIES=/media/sounds,/media/memes,/media/intros
```

Supported formats are AAC, FLAC, M4A, MP3, OGG, Opus, WAV, and WebM. New files
appear within a minute, or straight away after Refresh library; a container
restart is not required.

## Discord commands

- `/soundboard play` — choose and play a library sound
- `/soundboard youtube` — play a YouTube URL
- `/soundboard settings` — show or change this server's default volume and
  normalize (changing them requires the Manage Server permission)
- `/soundboard clip` — save the last minute of voice chat (recording must be on)
- `/soundboard stop` — stop playback
- `/soundboard leave` — disconnect the bot from voice

`play` and `youtube` accept optional `volume` (0–300%) and `normalize`
options. Anything left out uses the server defaults, which can also be set on
the web UI's Settings page.

The library is cached and rescanned at most once a minute, or immediately when
**Refresh library** is clicked.

The user issuing `play` or `youtube` must already be in a voice channel. Starting
a new item replaces the currently playing item for that server.

## Voice clips (experimental)

The bot can keep a rolling recording of its voice channel and save the last
minute on demand. Discord encrypts voice end to end, and stock discord.py
cannot receive it, so this uses
[discord-ext-voice-recv](https://github.com/zacker150/discord-ext-voice-recv),
which replaces discord.py with a fork. It is off by default.

1. Set `ENABLE_RECORDING=true` in `.env` and rerun `bash deploy.sh` to rebuild.
   If you run the published image instead of building it, use
   `ghcr.io/karlbuckle/soundbot:recording` in place of `:latest`.
2. Open **Clips**, choose a server and voice channel, and click
   **Start recording**. The bot joins and stays in that channel, and rejoins
   after restarts and dropped connections until recording is stopped.
3. Click **Save last minute** in the top bar (or run `/soundboard clip`).
   Clips are saved to the data volume (`/data/clips`) and can be played,
   downloaded, or deleted from the Clips page.

Only the last 60 seconds are kept in memory (`RECORDING_SECONDS` changes this);
nothing is written to disk until a clip is saved. Each speaker is recorded
separately, so their loudness is evened out automatically before mixing.
Per-person sliders on the Clips page fine-tune anyone who still sounds off.
Sounds played by the bot are included as their own "Soundboard" track.
**Disconnect** (or `/soundboard leave`) stops recording and clears the buffer.

Click **Edit** on a saved clip to trim it and change each person's level in
the browser. Drag across the waveform to choose the part to keep, preview it,
then save it as a new clip or replace the original. Clips keep one track per
speaker (in a `.stems` folder next to the clip) so levels can be changed after
saving; clips saved before this existed can be trimmed only.

Make sure everyone in the channel knows they may be recorded. To go back to
stock discord.py, set `ENABLE_RECORDING=false` and rerun `bash deploy.sh`.

## Local development

FFmpeg and Node.js must be installed and available on `PATH`. Node.js is used by
`yt-dlp` for current YouTube extraction; both dependencies are already included
in the Docker image.

### Explore locally without Docker or admin access

Once the portable test environment has been created in `.tools`, run:

```bat
run-local.cmd
```

This runs the tests, scans `./sounds`, opens the web UI at
`http://127.0.0.1:8000`, and leaves Discord disabled. Press Ctrl+C to stop it.
Pass a port as the first argument if 8000 is busy. For example,
`run-local.cmd 8080`. Add `skip-tests` as the second argument for a faster
restart: `run-local.cmd 8000 skip-tests`. Voice playback and YouTube extraction
still require FFmpeg (and current YouTube extraction may require Node.js).

```text
python -m venv .venv
.venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements-dev.txt
uvicorn app.main:app --reload
pytest
```

The app will still start without `DISCORD_TOKEN`, which is useful for working on
the UI, though playback and server selection will be unavailable.
